        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe audit logic is correctly isolated in its own single-responsibility class (`AuditOwnerCreated`) with a dedicated step and good javadoc \u2014 that part is clean and additive. Two structural deviations from this codebase's idiom:\n\n1. **`govern: [ transaction ]` on a pure-logging step.** In this repo transaction governance marks steps that do transactional DB work (`save`, `apply`). `AuditOwnerCreated` only writes a log line \u2014 it performs no persistence \u2014 so carrying `govern: [ transaction ]` is misleading: it signals the step participates in the write transaction when it does not, and it holds the DB transaction open across logging I/O for no reason. Per the worked example, the responder step (`respond`) is intentionally un-governed; the audit step belongs in that same un-governed tail. Drop the `govern:` line from the `audit:` entry.\n\n2. **Generic method name `service`.** Every other function class in this pipeline names its single public method after its verb (`validate`, `load`, `apply`, `save`, `respond`). Rename `service(...)` to `audit(...)` to match the codebase's convention and the class/step name. OfficeFloor requires exactly one public method either way, so there's no wiring cost.\n\nNeither affects where the logic lives (the class placement is right) \u2014 they're idiom/consistency fixes so a maintainer reads the step the same way as its siblings."
